## 4. The Normal Distribution: The Bell Curve

The continuous king of statistics:

@math label="eq:normal-pdf"
f(x) = \frac{1}{\sigma\sqrt{2\pi}}\, e^{-\frac{(x-\mu)^2}{2\sigma^2}}
@/math

Symmetric, bell-shaped, centred on the mean $\mu$, with spread set by
$\sigma$. Why it is everywhere: averaging many small independent
influences produces a bell — the **central limit theorem** — so
heights, measurement errors, and exam scores (raw or scaled) all
follow it.

### The 68–95–99.7 rule

| Range | Contains |
| --- | --- |
| $\mu \pm 1\sigma$ | ≈ 68% of data |
| $\mu \pm 2\sigma$ | ≈ 95% of data |
| $\mu \pm 3\sigma$ | ≈ 99.7% of data |

[tip]
Adult male heights average about 170 cm with $\sigma \approx 7$ cm.
So 95% of men stand between 156 and 184 cm — and a 200 cm man is
over 4σ tall: fewer than about 3 in 100,000.
[/tip]

### Standard scores (z-scores)

@math label="eq:z-score"
z = \frac{x - \mu}{\sigma}
@/math

The z-score counts how many standard deviations an observation lies
from the mean — a common currency for comparing across different
distributions. A student 1.5σ above the mean on either a physics or
a history exam has the same z = +1.5.

## 5. Sampling and the Central Limit Theorem

Take many random samples of size $n$ from *any* distribution with
finite variance; the sample means themselves are approximately
normal with standard deviation $\frac{\sigma}{\sqrt{n}}$ — the
**standard error**.

This is the engine of opinion polls: a sample of 1,000 shrinks the
margin of error to roughly $\frac{1}{\sqrt{1000}} \approx 3$% of the
population's spread, regardless of the population's shape.

[warning]
The bell curve's tails tempt overconfidence: the 2008 financial
crisis partly came from models assuming normally distributed (thin
tails) market moves, underestimating extreme events. Always ask
whether extreme outcomes are truly as rare as the normal model
claims.
[/warning]

## 6. Choosing the Right Model

| Situation | Model |
| --- | --- |
| Count successes in fixed trials, constant $p$ | Binomial |
| Counts of rare events in time/space (typos per page) | Poisson |
| Measurements, averages, natural quantities | Normal |
| Waiting time between random events | Exponential |

[summary]
Distributions spread probability over outcomes: describe them by
expected value $E(X) = \sum x_ip_i$ and standard deviation. The
binomial gives the probability of exactly $k$ successes in $n$
independent trials as $\mathrm{C}(n,k)p^k(1-p)^{n-k}$, with mean $np$.
The normal bell — universal via the central limit theorem — obeys
the 68–95–99.7 rule and standardises through z-scores, powering
polls and measurement alike. Real analysts pick (or question) the
model to fit the process generating the data.
[/summary]
